# Three roles, three connection strings. This separation is not stylistic — it is
# the mechanism that makes tenant isolation real (research.md D4).

# Migration / owner role. Runs DDL and OWNS the tables.
# Never used by the running application.
DATABASE_URL_MIGRATION=postgresql://lc_migration:lc_migration_dev@localhost:5432/legalconnect

# Application role. Owns NOTHING, is not superuser, holds no BYPASSRLS.
# PostgreSQL silently ignores RLS for superusers and for the table owner, so
# connecting as either would leave every policy written and the isolation
# nonexistent — with the test suite still green. src/main.ts asserts these
# attributes at startup and refuses to boot if they are wrong.
DATABASE_URL_APP=postgresql://lc_app:lc_app_dev@localhost:5432/legalconnect

# Platform administration role. Cross-tenant reach deliberately limited to the
# tenant, plan and audit_event tables — no business table (research.md D9).
DATABASE_URL_PLATFORM=postgresql://lc_platform:lc_platform_dev@localhost:5432/legalconnect

# Retention role. May drop audit partitions past the 24-month window (FR-019).
# The application deliberately does not hold this connection.
DATABASE_URL_RETENTION=postgresql://lc_retention:lc_retention_dev@localhost:5432/legalconnect

PORT=3000

# The platform administration surface authenticates nothing in this slice, so it is
# bound to loopback only and must not be exposed to a network before slice 003.
PLATFORM_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1

# --- Slice 002: Identity, Membership & Invitation --------------------------

# Transactional email (research.md D7). SES is unavailable in mx-central-1, so
# invitation delivery goes through SES in a secondary region rather than a
# third-party provider — this adds no vendor and reuses the same AWS IAM and
# Secrets Manager patterns already in place. Region is a plan.md decision, not
# a constitutional one; the value below is the candidate, not yet confirmed by
# infra (plan.md open item 2).
EMAIL_PROVIDER_REGION=us-east-1
EMAIL_SENDER_ADDRESS=no-reply@legalconnect.mx

# Enumeration-resistance thresholds (research.md D8). Both are configuration,
# not schema — the 256-bit invitation reference already makes brute force
# infeasible; these bound the log and refusal volume a scripted attempt would
# otherwise generate. See plan.md open item 1 for confirming/tuning these.
INVITATION_MAX_FAILED_ATTEMPTS=10
INVITATION_ISSUANCE_RATE_PER_HOUR=200

# --- Slice 007: Case Documents ----------------------------------------------

# Object storage for document content (research.md D6). Local dev points these at
# MinIO (docker-compose.yml's `minio` service) — an S3-API-compatible server that
# behaves identically to real S3 for signing and expiry, so isolation tests prove
# something real. Production points the same four values at real S3 in
# mx-central-1 (plan.md Constraints) — no code change, only these values.
# To use a real bucket, change only these six values (endpoint empty for AWS S3), then
# run `npm run check:storage` and restart the backend. The app finds an AWS bucket's real
# region on its own. Tests always use the local MinIO (tests/setup-env.ts), whatever is here.
OBJECT_STORE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000
OBJECT_STORE_REGION=us-east-1
OBJECT_STORE_BUCKET=legalconnect-documents-dev
OBJECT_STORE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=lc_minio_dev
OBJECT_STORE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=lc_minio_dev_password
# MinIO requires path-style addressing (bucket in the URL path, not a subdomain);
# real AWS S3 does not need this set.
OBJECT_STORE_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true
# 021 Decision 4: the largest single document upload, in bytes. Default 26214400 (25 MB);
# a larger file is refused 413 file_too_large before anything is stored.
DOCUMENT_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=26214400

# --- Slice 018: Client Screens ----------------------------------------------

# Origins allowed to call this API from a browser, comma-separated.
#
# 018 is the first slice where a browser talks to this API — 016a's shell makes no
# network calls — and the frontend runs on its own port, so every call is
# cross-origin. Without this the preflight is refused and the frontend reports an
# opaque network failure that says nothing about the cause.
#
# Never a wildcard, though 003 changed WHY. Until this slice the API trusted
# `x-identity-id` outright, so any allowed origin could read any tenant's data —
# a wildcard was catastrophic. That header is now gone (FR-041) and every request
# is validated against the session table, so a wildcard would no longer hand over
# tenant data on its own. It stays forbidden anyway: an allowed origin can still
# drive a browser that HOLDS a live session, and CORS is what keeps a page the
# person happens to be visiting from acting as them.
#
# Outside production an unset value defaults to the frontend's dev origin; in
# production an unset value allows no browser origin at all.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# --- Slice 003: Authentication & MFA ----------------------------------------
#
# EVERY VALUE IN THIS BLOCK IS A PARAMETER, NOT A SWITCH.
#
# None of them can disable second-factor enrollment or the sign-in challenge, and
# none may ever be made able to. Constitution v1.5.0 (Authentication) forbids the
# existence of "any configuration flag, environment variable, plan entitlement,
# per-tenant setting or feature flag that disables MFA enrollment or the MFA
# challenge" — a mechanism whose only purpose is to switch this off must not exist
# to be misused, misconfigured or wrongly defaulted. Adding one here is a
# constitution violation, not a configurable trade-off.
#
# `tests/integration/mfa-no-disable-path.test.ts` asserts this exhaustively rather
# than by sampling: it inspects every variable in this file, not a chosen subset
# (FR-007, SC-004). A new key added here is inspected by that test automatically.

# Fifth connection string, for the `lc_auth` role (tasks.md T000's resolution).
#
# `lc_auth` is the ONLY role holding grants on `identity_credential`,
# `identity_factor` and `backup_code`. `lc_app` holds none — not a narrowed grant,
# none — so the application's ordinary connection cannot reach authentication
# material at all. That is a data-layer guarantee, not an application-layer one, and
# it is what `tests/integration/auth-grants-lockdown.test.ts` proves.
#
# Used by `src/modules/auth/` and by nothing else. Like every other role here it
# owns nothing, is not superuser and holds no BYPASSRLS.
DATABASE_URL_AUTH=postgresql://lc_auth:lc_auth_dev@localhost:5432/legalconnect

# Where the envelope key for TOTP secrets comes from — `local` or `kms`
# (research.md D5). A TOTP secret cannot be hashed: it MUST be readable on every
# verification, which makes it the most sensitive recoverable material in the
# database. The constitution therefore requires it encrypted at rest "under an
# application-held key that is separate from the database".
#
# `local` is for development and CI ONLY. `src/main.ts` carries a startup assertion
# that REFUSES TO BOOT when a deployed environment resolves the local provider — an
# assertion, not a warning (T027). The port exists because the AWS account blockage
# may be account-wide, in which case KMS is unreachable too; hard-wiring KMS would
# reintroduce the halt the v1.5.0 amendment removed.
AUTH_KEY_PROVIDER=local

# 32-byte key, base64. DEV AND CI ONLY — never a deployed environment.
# The value below is a throwaway generated for this file and protects nothing.
AUTH_LOCAL_KEY=ZGV2LW9ubHktMzItYnl0ZS1rZXktbm90LXNlY3JldCE=

# Required when AUTH_KEY_PROVIDER=kms. Empty in dev. Access to this key is
# restricted and audited to the same standard the constitution sets for PAC/CSD
# credentials, including step-up MFA on operations that touch it (FR-016).
AUTH_KMS_KEY_ID=

# Consecutive failed second-factor attempts before an identity is locked (FR-021).
# The lockout is the security control; the per-origin throttle is best-effort and
# explicitly not authoritative, because it cannot be across ECS tasks with no Redis.
AUTH_LOCKOUT_THRESHOLD=5

# How long the lockout lasts, in minutes. It lifts on its own — no administrative
# action, so a locked-out person is not a support ticket (FR-021, SC-032).
AUTH_LOCKOUT_MINUTES=15

# Backup codes issued per set (FR-031). Re-issuance replaces the entire set rather
# than topping it up, and there is no path that returns a set a second time.
AUTH_BACKUP_CODE_COUNT=10
